    <comment>New package: soapy-fcdpp - the SoapySDR hardware backend for the FUNcube
Dongle Pro+ (and the older Pro V1.x).

Why Factory: Factory already ships soapy-sdr plus seven SoapySDR modules
(soapy-airspy, soapy-bladerf, soapy-hackrf, soapy-osmo, soapy-remote,
soapy-rtlsdr, soapy-uhd). The FUNcube Dongle is a common entry-level
receiver and its backend is the one obvious hole in that set, so SoapySDR
frontends already in Factory - gqrx and gr-osmosdr - cannot drive the
hardware today. The package has been maintained in hardware:sdr since 2018
and builds there for Tumbleweed, Factory:ARM and Leap 16.0.

License: BSL-1.0. LICENSE_1_0.txt, debian/copyright (Files: *) and the
per-file SPDX-License-Identifier headers all agree. The tag previously read
MIT, which was simply wrong; it is corrected here and the licence file is
now installed via %license. A Legal-Review-Notice above the License tag
records why fcd.c and fcd.h look different to a scanner: they carry only
Xcode's &quot;All rights reserved&quot; template line, from the same author.

No proprietary dependency: the FUNcube Dongle is an ALSA-audio-plus-HID
device, so the module links libSoapySDR, libasound and libhidapi-libusb and
nothing else. There is no vendor SDK and no firmware blob - every file in
the source archive is plain text.

Snapshot rather than a tag: upstream has tagged exactly twice, 0.1.0 and
0.1.1, both in 2019, and cut no releases at all, while master is 43 commits
ahead and upstream's own Changelog.txt calls that line &quot;Release 0.2.0
(pending)&quot;. This pins commit 1ae85f008f60 (2025-10-09) reproducibly via
obs_scm. Those commits add FUNcube Dongle Pro V1.x support, bias-T and TCXO
control, mmap direct-buffer streaming and a configurable period; they fix
device detection against the current libhidapi-libusb device-path format,
which the 2019 tag predates; and they fix the missing return value we
previously carried downstream as SoapyFCDPP-add-missing-return.patch, now
dropped. Factory's own soapy-sdr is likewise a pinned git snapshot.

pluskalm has been added as package maintainer in hardware:sdr.

ADDED IN THIS REVISION
Explicit &quot;Requires: soapy-sdr&quot; on the module subpackage, at dimstar's request
on SR 1374694 (soapy-plutosdr), applied to all five plugin submissions: &quot;Shouldn't that require soapy-sdr? The module
on its own seems quite useless&quot;.

To be precise about what this does and does not change: the dlopen itself lives
in libSoapySDR (SoapySDR::loadModules(), reached from Device::make()), which the
module already pulled in through its automatic soname dependency on
libSoapySDR.so.0.8-3 - so any application linking the library could always load
the module. What nothing pulled in was the soapy-sdr package, which ships
SoapySDRUtil; the module could therefore be installed on a system with no
SoapySDR client at all to enumerate or probe it. So this is a usability
dependency rather than a functional one, and the spec comment says so. The
soname dependency remains the ABI-exact constraint, which is why the new line is
deliberately unversioned instead of duplicating the ABI in the spec.

Stated plainly so it is not mistaken for convention: none of Factory's existing
SoapySDR plugins - soapy-airspy, soapy-bladerf, soapy-hackrf, soapy-osmo,
soapy-remote, soapy-rtlsdr, soapy-uhd - carries such a Requires. This change
makes these five stricter than current Factory practice; it is what the reviewer
asked for, not a convention being matched.

Rebuilt natively on aarch64 after the change: green, rpmlint unchanged, and
&quot;rpm -qp --requires&quot; on the built module now lists both
libSoapySDR.so.0.8-3()(64bit) and soapy-sdr.

Supersedes SR 1374707.</comment>
